# Line endings: LF in the repository AND in every working tree, on every platform.
#
# This is a correctness requirement, not a style preference. Two mechanisms compare
# sha256 hashes of bundled seed files against hashes that are ultimately derived from
# git blob content (always LF):
#
#   - the seed-content refresh's provenance table, which decides whether a bundled
#     dashboard on a user's disk is pristine (safe to update) or user-edited, and
#   - the legacy recipe-hash backfill (app/seed_refresh/legacy_recipe_hashes.py),
#     generated from release tags to recognise untouched pre-provenance dashboards.
#
# A checkout with core.autocrlf=true (the Git-for-Windows default) would hand the
# build CRLF copies of exactly those files. Every hash comparison then fails, the
# backfill silently misses, and users' untouched demo dashboards never receive
# bundle updates again — with no error anywhere. Pinning eol=lf makes a bundle built
# on Windows byte-identical to one built on macOS or Linux.
#
# See dev-docs/seed-content-refresh.md §10.1.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The bundled ledgers are hashed seed content — spelled out because they matter most.
*.beancount text eol=lf

# Binary assets: excluded explicitly so `text=auto` never has to guess.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
